s because  jenny's role in the show is either uh to  act as a worse version of dan or as a  worse version of blair but this serves  to demonstrate what the diminishing  returns property is that basically the  more characters you add to your set the  less the benefit of adding bl of adding  jenny would be that either they are in  that either some character you add is in  scenes with jenny that the other  characters aren't in in which case the  benefit of adding jenny goes down or  they're not in which case the benefit  stays the same but the benefit would  never go up and so that's the key idea  behind sub-modular functions  so  now that we've identified a function  that we could use uh and seen what the  diminishing returns property is how do  we actually optimize this  i'm going to demonstrate this using uh  just a general class of functions called  the feature-based functions the one we  described is a feature-based function  this is a general class of submodular  functions  imagine that you have some data set  where each row is an example and each  column is a feature what you would do  because this is a greedy algorithm is  you would start with the first example  and you would try to measure the  function value given it  well you take that  the first thing that you do in this  function is you sum down the columns of  all of the selected examples well you  haven't selected anything yet and so the  sum is just going to be the future  values for that first example  then you apply fee which is any type of  concave function generally i use square  root because it's fast but it's any  function that is estab that is uh has a  diminishing returns aspect to it  so you apply that function and then you  sum along the resulting vector and you  get some value and that is the value of  you know the set that you've put in  now let's say you scan through all the  items in this data set and you've  determined that the first item is the  best one and you're trying to determine  the second item to add and you're  considering this purple one well now you  you do your sum as normal which are  going to be the sum of the purple  feature values and the orange feature  values note here that there are two  purple feature values that have the same  uh they have the same value uh this  feature here where orange also has a  high feature value and this one here  where orange has a low feature value  when you apply your uh your function  with diminishing returns what you'll  notice is that the benefit of adding the  purple example to this feature that  already had a lot of it a lot observed  is small but the same feature value is  much higher when you haven't observed  the feature this gives these functions a  property where it's trying to choose  examples where you were seeing features  that you didn't see in previous examples  so this is good when your features  represent different quantity qualities  of the data  and the higher value corresponds to  having more of that quality so this  works for things like set functions or  when you have things like word counts or  if you have posterior probabilities or  labels or like a kernel matrix or  something like that it's not going to  work well for things like xy coordinates  so here you might get some you know  additional value and then you choose the  example with the highest gain given the  first feature that you the first example  that you already saw now like all good  talks let's return to gossip girl  imagine  uh so in this um  in this show as you all know there are  these text message blasts that are set  out by this character known as gossip  girl just in time to stir up trouble  amongst all the main characters the  first one in the show reads like this  spotted lonely boy can't believe the  love of his life was returned if only  she knew who he was but everyone knows  serena and everyone is talking wonder  what blair waldorf thinks sir they're  bffs but we always thought their  boyfriend nate had 