s generally  proceed that they choose a first example  that is has as high feature values and  as many features as possible and then  iteratively choose subsequent examples  that have high feature values and  features that were not observed before  in contrast to feature-based functions  another broad class of some modular  functions or the graph based functions  these ones don't operate on examples you  know that have featurization but rather  they take in a graph like a network or  you know like a social network or an  interaction network transit or something  like that  facility location is probably the most  famous graph based uh function and you  know actually the most widely used  submodular function that exists it's  literally used to locate facilities thus  the name you can also think about it  like a greedy version of the k-meat wage  algorithm or it's also known as the  k-means plus plus initialization  the idea here is that you want to choose  centroids that are very similar to  uh to very simple that are very similar  to examples that are not well  represented by the already existing  centroids  so  to give you an example um i was sitting  in an airport one day and i had the  delightful gift of having my flight  delayed by several hours and as a tech  worker with no understanding of how a  domain works i thought i was perfectly  suited to solve this optimization  problem so i thought why don't i just  download a whole bunch of data from open  flights and try to figure out where  airlines should place their hubs in  order to make my life more convenient so  here are all the airports that are  located in the united states according  to open flights and when you connect  them you see a bunch of hubs starting to  emerge this is by no means all of the  flights that occur in the united states  these are just the ones that were in the  open flight database at the time that i  um  downloaded them  if you take the number of airports if  you take the airports that